.TH RUNEVM 1 "2026" "runevm 0.3.0" "Rune Manual"
.SH NAME
runevm \- run Rune bytecode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B runevm
.RI [ options ]
.I file.rbc
.RI [ args " ...]"
.br
.B runevm \-\-restore
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B runevm
loads a bytecode file produced by
.BR rune (1)
and interprets it. Everything after
.I file.rbc
is passed to the program and is what its
.B CommandLine.arguments
returns;
.B CommandLine.name
is
.I file.rbc
as spelled on the command line.
.PP
The VM is a copying collector over two semispaces. The heap grows on demand, so
.B \-\-heap\-size
only sets how much is mapped up front.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-size " N"
Map
.I N
bytes per semispace initially (default 4194304, minimum 4096).
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the bytecode of the program and exit without running it.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Trace every instruction executed to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print the number of collections, bytes allocated, semispace size and live bytes
to standard error at exit.
.TP
.B \-\-count
Print the instructions executed and the bytes and objects allocated to standard
error at exit. The numbers are the same for every run of a program.
.TP
.BI \-\-heap\-fill " P"
Grow the heap after a collection until at most
.I P
percent of it is in use, 1 to 100 (default 50). A lower
.I P
makes fewer collections for more memory.
.TP
.BI \-\-gc\-stress " N"
Collect before every
.IR N th
allocation. This exercises the collector and the primitives' handling of heap
pointers, and makes programs far slower.
.TP
.B \-\-checked
Make each
.B DECON
test the constructor tag it is given, and stop the program where the value
has another. A match that names every constructor of a datatype does not test
the last, so this finds a compiler that got a tag wrong; the test suites run
so.
.TP
.B \-\-emulate\-fork
Fork as on Windows, which has no
.BR fork (2):
by a second
.B runevm
that is handed this one's state. This tests that path where the system has a
fork of its own.
.TP
.BI \-\-resume " TOKEN"
Carry on as the child of such a fork.
.B runevm
passes this to the second VM itself; it is not for use by hand.
.TP
.BI \-\-restore " FILE"
Carry on the world that
.B Runtime.save
wrote to
.IR FILE ,
from that call, which returns
.B Restored
this time. No
.I file.rbc
is given: the image carries its program.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B runevm
exits with the status the program passed to
.BR OS.Process.exit ,
0 when the program returns normally, and 1 after reporting an uncaught
exception on standard error. It exits 2 when the options are wrong, when the
bytecode file cannot be read, and on a fatal error in the bytecode itself
(a type error the compiler should have prevented).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rune (1)
